WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.450
The size of the tide isn’t the same every week.

2
00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:05.525
It follows the moon.

3
00:00:05.775 --> 00:00:07.600
Look at the diagram.

4
00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:13.100
At new moon and full moon, the sun and the moon are lined up, and they pull together.

5
00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:16.650
That gives spring tides, with the biggest range.

6
00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.750
Spring has nothing to do with the season.

7
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:22.225
Think of the water springing up.

8
00:00:22.475 --> 00:00:28.725
At the first and third quarter moons, the sun and the moon pull at right angles and partly cancel.

9
00:00:28.975 --> 00:00:32.350
That gives neap tides, with the smallest range.

10
00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:38.775
So when do you get the lowest low water of the month, the kind that leaves the flats dry?

11
00:00:39.025 --> 00:00:42.225
On a spring tide, around new or full moon.

12
00:00:42.475 --> 00:00:51.450
And when a spring tide falls on the moon’s closest approach to the earth, you get what people call a king tide, the kind that puts water in the streets.

13
00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:53.675
Wind matters too.

14
00:00:53.925 --> 00:00:57.150
Onshore wind and low pressure pile water up.

15
00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.650
Offshore wind pushes it out.
